question:
On a round table, 14 labeled seats are arranged in a circle. A seating pattern is a binary sequence (b_1, b_2, ..., b_14) with b_i in {0,1}, where adjacency is taken cyclically (i.e., pairs (b_i, b_{i+1}) for i=1,...,14-1 and also (b_14, b_{1})). Among all such binary sequences, consider those that simultaneously satisfy the following:
- exactly 10 entries are 1;
- exactly 3 reverse orders occur (adjacent pair 1 followed by 0);
- exactly 3 forward orders occur (adjacent pair 0 followed by 1);
- every run of consecutive 1s has length at most 4;
- every run of consecutive 0s has length at most 5.
Positions are labeled, so rotations are considered different. How many such sequences exist?

On a round table, 22 labeled seats are arranged in a circle. A seating pattern is a binary sequence (b_1, b_2, ..., b_22) with b_i in {0,1}, where adjacency is taken cyclically (i.e., pairs (b_i, b_{i+1}) for i=1,...,22-1 and also (b_22, b_{1})). Among all such binary sequences, consider those that simultaneously satisfy the following:
- exactly 11 entries are 1;
- exactly 3 reverse orders occur (adjacent pair 1 followed by 0);
- exactly 3 forward orders occur (adjacent pair 0 followed by 1);
- every run of consecutive 1s has length at most 4;
- every run of consecutive 0s has length at most 5.
Positions are labeled, so rotations are considered different. How many such sequences exist?
